<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fall of Constantinople</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1453">1453</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as conquest of Constantinople · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-ottoman-wars">Byzantine–Ottoman wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> was the capture of the Byzantine capital by the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> in 1453, after a siege in which heavy artillery had a large part. It ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and made the city the Ottoman capital. The Ottoman recovery that made it possible followed the empire's defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> in 1402, a link noted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> profile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="Fall of Constantinople" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DimiTalen · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siege_of_Constantinople_fresco,_Moldovi%C8%9Ba_monastery,_Vatra_Moldovi%C8%9Bei,_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> had been an imperial capital since 330 and had been captured only once in eleven centuries, in the Sack of Constantinople during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1204. The Nicaeans retook it in 1261 and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a>. Between 1346 and 1349 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> killed almost half the inhabitants. By 1453 the city was a series of walled villages separated by fields, encircled by the fifth-century Theodosian walls.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> succeeded his father in 1451 at the age of 19, and many European courts expected little of him. In early 1452 he began building the fortress of Rumeli Hisarı on the European shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>, which with the older Anadolu Hisarı controlled sea traffic. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI Palaiologos</a> asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> for help, but union of the eastern and western churches, agreed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Florence</a> in 1439, met strong resistance in the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> stretched a chain across the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a> and repaired the land walls. The defenders numbered under 8,000 in all. George Sphrantzes recorded 4,773 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and 200 foreigners, with up to three thousand Genoese, Venetians and others who came to help, to guard a perimeter of twelve miles. The siege began on 6 April 1453. Mehmed's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> fired on the walls for weeks, but their slow rate of fire let the Byzantines repair most of the damage after each shot.</p><p>On 28 May processions were held and the emperor attended a last Vespers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>. The final assault came on 29 May, after a siege of 53 days. The walls had protected the city for 800 years, but Ottoman infantry with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombard">bombards</a> overcame them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Mehmed promised his soldiers three days of plunder, but later sources say he rode into the city on the first day and halted the looting. By 2 June he found the city largely deserted and half in ruins. Mehmed let Christians who had escaped capture or been ransomed return to their homes, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> the Ottoman capital in place of Adrianople. He called himself Kayser-i Rum, Caesar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><p>The fall ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, which had lasted nearly 1,500 years, and many modern historians treat it as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Byzantine scholars who migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> are considered by many to have been key to the revival of Greek and Roman studies behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanism. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> became official only in 1930.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fall of Constantinople” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In church affairs, Moscow rejected the union with Rome agreed at the Council of Florence in 1439, and in 1448 Russian bishops chose Jonah as metropolitan without approval from Constantinople, a de facto independence confirmed after the Ottoman <b>conquest of Constantinople</b> in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mehmed II completed the <b>conquest of Constantinople</b> on 29 May 1453 after a siege of some fifty-three days, in which great bronze cannon cast by the founder Urban breached the Theodosian walls that had resisted assault for over a thousand years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>More scholars came west before and after the Ottoman <b>conquest of Constantinople</b> in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…battered the land walls of Constantinople, which no army had breached in more than a thousand years, with an artillery train that included enormous bombards cast by the founder Orban, who had first offered his services to the Byzantines; the <b>city fell</b> after a siege of fifty-three days.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ottoman <b>conquest of Constantinople</b>, Mehmed II took Old and New Phocaea in 1455, ending Genoese control of the alum trade until new mines were found at Tolfa in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-phocaea">Lordship of Phocaea</a></li></ul></section>
